declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 15, $j$4.3 First and Second Thessalonians: The Hope of the Lord's Return$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "1 Thessalonians 4 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/1Thessalonians/4"}, {"title": "1 Thessalonians 5 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/1Thessalonians/5"}, {"title": "2 Thessalonians 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/2Thessalonians/3"}], "body": "Read 1 Thessalonians 4, 1 Thessalonians 5 and 2 Thessalonians 3 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThessalonica was the capital of Macedonia. Paul preached there on his second journey, and a church was born, but he had to leave quickly because of opposition. Soon after, he wrote 1 and 2 Thessalonians to encourage the young believers. These are among his earliest letters. Paul comforted those who had lost loved ones by teaching that Jesus will return, that the dead in Christ will rise first, and that believers will be with the Lord forever. He also gave short commands for daily life: rejoice evermore, pray without ceasing and in every thing give thanks. In the second letter he told the believers not to be weary in well doing and to keep working while they wait.\n\nKey words: hope, return, comfort, thanksgiving, faithful.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell what hope Paul gave in 1 Thessalonians 4:13 to 18. (2) List the three short commands in 1 Thessalonians 5:16 to 18. (3) Explain why Paul told the Thessalonians not to be weary in well doing.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Today we look at the two letters Paul wrote to the church at Thessalonica. Thessalonica was a busy port city and the capital of Macedonia. Paul preached there for only a short time on his second journey, as Acts 17 tells us, and a mob chased him out of town. He worried about the young believers, so he sent Timothy to check on them, and then he wrote them a letter. These are some of the first letters Paul ever wrote.\n\nThe Thessalonians had a question. Some of their loved ones had died, and they wondered what would happen to them. Paul answered with hope. First Thessalonians 4:13 says, But I would not have you to be ignorant, brethren, concerning them which are asleep, that ye sorrow not, even as others which have no hope. Christians do grieve, but we do not grieve like people without hope.\n\nThen Paul described the return of the Lord. Verse 16 says, For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: Verse 17 says, Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord. And verse 18 says, Wherefore comfort one another with these words. The promise of the Lord's return is meant to comfort us.\n\nPaul also gave short commands for daily life in chapter 5. First Thessalonians 5:16 says, Rejoice evermore. Verse 17 says, Pray without ceasing. Verse 18 says, In every thing give thanks: for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you. And verse 21 says, Prove all things; hold fast that which is good. Joy, prayer and thanksgiving are the will of God for every believer.\n\nIn Second Thessalonians Paul wrote again, because some believers thought the Lord's coming was so near that they had stopped working. Paul reminded them in chapter 3 and verse 3, But the Lord is faithful, who shall stablish you, and keep you from evil. He also said in verse 10, For even when we were with you, this we commanded you, that if any would not work, neither should he eat. Waiting for the Lord does not mean being idle. And verse 13 says, But ye, brethren, be not weary in well doing. We keep serving faithfully until He comes.\n\nYour task is to tell what hope Paul gave in 1 Thessalonians 4, list the three short commands in 1 Thessalonians 5:16 to 18, and explain why Paul told the church not to be weary in well doing. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe Lord is coming, so we comfort one another and keep working. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.3 First and Second Thessalonians: The Hope of the Lord's Return$j$);
end
$seed$;
